{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787774739,"nonce":"g6RmlqXosTeccdrRzHY2A379bcE=","title":"feat(connections): Forgejo onboarding + declarative connections — ADR-0060 slices 4, 5, 6","message":"\u003e Imported from pull request #82 (MERGED)\n\u003e Branch: `integrate/adr-0060-4-5-6` → `main`\n\u003e Created: 2026-07-25T19:09:46Z · Merged: 2026-07-25T19:11:02Z\n\nCloses git-bug `8b3cf79` (slice 4), `b48df22` (slice 5), `121a16b` (slice 6). **Completes ADR-0060.**\n\nBuilt as two parallel branches and integrated here, because 4/5 and 6 both rewrite the same connection surfaces. Merging them separately would have left the gap described at the bottom.\n\nSlices 4+5. `POST /v1/connections` writes the token through to `SecretProvider` under a server-generated `_forge` `credential_ref` — **before** the row is written, so a failure leaves a harmless orphan secret rather than a live row that can't authenticate — then bind/unbind/webhook endpoints, and a pick-list backed by a Forgejo `list_accessible_repos`.\n\n**The claim that matters** (`onboarding_a_forgejo_repo_creates_a_project_whose_pushes_run`): create connection → pick-list → bind → hook recorded → `\"project\":\"web\"` on `GET /v1/repos` → a **real HMAC-signed Forgejo push delivery** → 202 with `run_ids` → run durable in the DB. That's the path GitHub always had and Forgejo never did — remember a Project *is* a `forge_repos` binding, so binding is what brings it into existence.\n\n`POST /v1/connections` **rejects `github` with a 400 naming the App**: installing it *is* registration, so a create form there could only lie.\n\n1. **`ForgejoForge::register_webhook` set no `config.secret`** — the body was `{url, content_type}` only, so every delivery from a Scarab-registered hook would have been rejected 401 by our own `/webhooks/forgejo`. Registration reports success; nothing ever runs.\n2. **`RegistryForge` never implemented `list_accessible_repos`** — it inherited the port default, so slice 3's re-sync endpoint **could only ever 501 in production** while the UI offered the button. That's my bug from #77: the error was honest, but I gated the button on `kind == GitHub` instead of on actual capability. Fixed via a connection-scoped `ForgeAdapters` port — needed anyway, since onboarding asks questions of a connection with *nothing bound*, so repo-routed `resolve()` has nothing to route on.\n\nSlice 6. `SCARAB_CONNECTIONS[_FILE]` / Helm `scarab.connections`, `deny_unknown_fields` so a typo fails the boot instead of being ignored.\n\n- **Ownership is durable** — migration `0038` adds `forge_connections.owned_by_config`. Only a persisted marker lets a later boot tell \"the row I provisioned\" from \"a row a human created\".\n- **Collision** (config declares an id the DB owns) → **boot refusal before any write**, naming both sources.\n- **Undeclaring releases ownership, it does not delete** — Projects and their Environments/secrets/RBAC hang off the bindings. Same restraint as re-sync.\n- **Credential resolution collapsed to one path**: overrides (per-connection config + the kind-wide App PEM as just another entry) → `SecretProvider`. The forge router, the startup audit and the Settings `credential_present` readout now share one table, so they cannot disagree.\n\nOne deliberate deviation from the ticket: an unregistered `credential.secret_ref` is a **DEGRADED warning, not a boot refusal** — only the running server can PUT that secret, so refusing would deadlock a fresh DB. Recorded in ADR part D.\n\nSlices 4/5 added five endpoints; slice 6 added ownership. Built in parallel, **every one of those endpoints arrived unguarded** — a UI write would land on a config-owned row and be reverted by the next deploy. Slice 6 spotted it and left `is_config_owned` ready; the final commit wires it into all four mutating endpoints (409 naming where the change belongs) and leaves reads open, since seeing a config-owned connection is how an admin confirms a deploy landed.\n\n`every_mutating_endpoint_refuses_a_config_owned_connection` is the regression guard. Verified red without the guards — `delete_connection` answered its *bound-repos* 409 instead, which is why the test asserts on the message and not just the status.\n\nTwo conflicts resolved by keeping both sides: `supports_resync` = (adapter wired \u0026\u0026 kind can enumerate) **AND NOT** config-owned; `RegistryForge` keeps slice 6's override table **and** slice 5's Forgejo webhook secret.\n\n- **Full workspace suite: 633 passed, 21 ignored, 124 suites** with `SCARAB_TEST_REQUIRE_PG=1` (one run on the integrated tree, per instruction)\n- `connections.rs` 19 tests · `config_connections.rs` 7 · 13 config unit tests · 1 PG ownership test\n- `openapi.json` + TS client regenerated; typecheck clean; `npm test` 72 passed\n- Slice 6 boot-verified for real against Postgres: both connections provisioned, audit skipped the env-overridden one and flagged the `secret_ref` one DEGRADED, second boot idempotent, collision refused\n- Settings UI driven in mock mode: GitHub row observe-only with no *Add repository*; Forgejo row with per-repo webhook retry and a pick-list that marks already-added repos and states the consequence\n- `helm template` default render **byte-identical**\n\n- **No live Forgejo instance was touched.** `/user/repos` and whether a real Forgejo honours `config.secret` are asserted against unit tests of the shapes we *believe* it uses. This is the ticket's stated follow-up and it is exactly where a wrong guess hides.\n- No live `helm install` (template/lint only); `DELETE …?unbind_repos=true` relies on the PG `ON DELETE CASCADE` by inspection, not by test.\n- ADR-0060 status stays `Proposed` — worth flipping to `Accepted` once this lands.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","files":null}]}